      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-012-shared-components.md</file>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-021-fluent-design-system.md</file>
      <file>docs/adr/ADR-038-testing-strategy.md</file>
      <file>.claude/patterns/auth/spaarke-sso-binding.md</file>
    </files>
    <patterns>
      <pattern name="follow task 006's fix vocabulary" location="projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/notes/typecheck-fix-patterns.md">Task 006 creates this file and records a canonical fix shape per error code. Read it before starting and follow the same shapes, appending your own section for anything it does not cover. Three concurrent tasks producing three different idioms for the same diagnostic makes the combined diff unreviewable.</pattern>
      <pattern name="exactOptionalPropertyTypes — the two correct fixes" location="src/client/office-addins/tsconfig.json line 21">`prop?: T` means "may be absent", NOT "may be undefined". Either widen the DECLARATION to `prop?: T | undefined` where the property genuinely may hold undefined, or fix the CALL SITE with a conditional spread such as `...(value !== undefined ? { prop: value } : {})`. In adapter option objects and fetch init objects the call-site spread is usually the honest fix, because the underlying API distinguishes an absent option from an explicit undefined one.</pattern>
      <pattern name="noUncheckedIndexedAccess — narrow, do not assert" location="src/client/office-addins/tsconfig.json line 20">An index access yields `T | undefined`. Narrow with a guard, a `??` default, optional chaining, or destructuring with a default — never `!`. This matters more here than anywhere else in the package: an unchecked index inside an adapter runs in an Office webview where a thrown TypeError shows the user a blank pane.</pattern>
      <pattern name="noUnusedParameters in adapter signatures" location="src/client/office-addins/shared/adapters/IHostAdapter.ts">Adapter methods implement a shared interface, so an implementation that legitimately ignores a parameter still must keep it positionally. Prefix with an underscore; do NOT delete it and do NOT change the interface signature — the interface is a contract that WordAdapter, OutlookAdapter and (after task 010) the factory all depend on, and task 013 will extend it with `getDocumentUrl()`.</pattern>
      <pattern name="displacement check" location="projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/notes/typecheck-baseline.md">006, 007 and 008 run concurrently on the same package. At the end of this task, re-run the full typecheck and compare the counts for shared/taskpane, word and outlook against the task 001 baseline rows. They must not increase. A change to an exported type in shared/adapters or shared/services is the most likely way to push errors into someone else's directory — if that happens, fix it inside yours or escalate.</pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Context + rigor declaration">Emit the mandatory rigor declaration (RIGOR LEVEL: FULL, reason, protocol steps). Load projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/CLAUDE.md, spec.md FR-18 and FR-04, plan.md finding F-e, and projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/notes/typecheck-baseline.md. Record this task's starting error counts and the baseline counts for the directories you do NOT own. Confirm whether task 001 reported the UNASSIGNED bucket as confined to shared/__mocks__/**.</step>
    <step order="1">Ensure src/client/office-addins/node_modules exists. If not, run `npm install --legacy-peer-deps --no-audit --no-fund` (a bare install fails with ERESOLVE), then revert package-lock.json if npm rewrote it.</step>
    <step order="2">Read word/WordHostAdapter.ts and plan.md finding F-e BEFORE touching WordAdapter.ts, so you know exactly which parts of the current WordAdapter are deliberately broken-and-owned-by-task-010. You are compiling that code, not correcting it.</step>
    <step order="3">Read projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/notes/typecheck-fix-patterns.md if task 006 has already created it, and adopt its fix shapes.</step>
    <step order="4">Work the errors file by file, highest count first per the task 001 breakdown, across shared/adapters/** then shared/services/** (then shared/__mocks__/** if it is yours per the constraint). Apply one canonical fix shape per error code, consistently.</step>
    <step order="5">Re-run `npm run typecheck` and filter to the directories you own. Iterate until that filter is empty.</step>
    <step order="6">Run the displacement check: re-run the full typecheck and compare the per-directory counts for shared/taskpane, word and outlook against the task 001 baseline. None may increase. Record the comparison.</step>
    <step order="7">Run `npm run build` (the production build; there is no build:prod) and confirm it is green.</step>
    <step order="8">Run `npm test` and confirm green, with the adapter and ApiClient suites still executing at their baseline counts — no test deleted, skipped or weakened.</step>
    <step order="9">Execute the ui-tests below and record each result, including the dark-mode check. The adapters and auth service sit under every pane interaction, so a regression here shows up as a failure to sign in or a failure to save rather than as a visual defect — the tests below are shaped accordingly.</step>
    <step order="10">Append your section to projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/notes/typecheck-fix-patterns.md (create it if task 006 has not yet), headed with this task's id. Record explicitly that the adapter seam was left structurally unchanged for task 010, and note anything you saw in WordAdapter.ts that task 010 should know.</step>
    <step order="10.5">Run the Step 9.5 quality gates (code-review plus adr-check) per the FULL rigor level. Direct code-review at two questions: did any edit change behaviour, and did the adapter seam change shape. Direct adr-check at ADR-028 in particular — it is arch-test enforced on this surface.</step>
    <step order="11">Update projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/tasks/TASK-INDEX.md: set this task's status to completed.</step>
    <step order="12">Document any deviation from this task file in projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/notes/.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If a typecheck error in your directories can only be resolved by editing a file under shared/taskpane/**, word/** or outlook/**, STOP and escalate per root CLAUDE.md section 6. Tasks 006 and 008 own those files and are running concurrently; reaching across produces a merge conflict and silently discards one of the two fixes.</trigger>
    <trigger>If a typecheck error can only be resolved by restructuring the adapter seam — porting `getCompressedFile()`, activating `HostAdapterFactory`, changing which adapter a taskpane constructs, or altering the IHostAdapter contract — STOP and escalate. That work is task 010's, is prescriptive-mode for a documented reason (plan.md finding F-e), and doing part of it here leaves task 010 with a half-migrated seam and no clean starting point.</trigger>
    <trigger>If a type error tempts a change that would put MSAL construction, `createNestablePublicClientApplication`, or a client secret into this package, STOP and escalate. ADR-028 is arch-test enforced; this is a security-sensitive boundary under root CLAUDE.md section 6 and is never a local judgment call.</trigger>
    <trigger>If a test file's type error reveals that the test asserts behaviour the adapter or client no longer has, STOP and escalate rather than deleting, skipping or rewriting the assertion (ADR-038 KEEP-path rules). These suites are the safety net task 010 depends on.</trigger>
    <trigger>If task 001 reported an UNASSIGNED bucket containing anything other than shared/__mocks__/**, STOP and request an ownership assignment rather than absorbing it. An unowned erroring file means FR-18's clean-typecheck criterion cannot be met by 006 plus 007 plus 008 alone.</trigger>
    <trigger>If clearing these directories requires adding, removing or upgrading an npm dependency (including a missing `@types` package), or relaxing any tsconfig option, STOP and escalate.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <ui-tests>
    <test name="Sign-in still works after auth-service type remediation">
      <steps>
        <step>Run `npm run build` in src/client/office-addins, then load the add-in in a dev build (`npm run start:outlook`).</step>
        <step>Open the Spaarke task pane while signed out, and complete sign-in.</step>
        <step>Confirm the pane transitions past the SignInView to the authenticated shell.</step>
      </steps>
      <expected>Sign-in completes via NAA exactly as before. No AADSTS error, no popup loop, and no new console exception. This is the primary regression signal for shared/services/AuthService.ts — a type change there fails here rather than visually.</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="Save path still reaches the BFF after adapter and ApiClient type remediation">
      <steps>
        <step>With the pane authenticated in Outlook, select an item and run a save through the Save view.</step>
        <step>Observe the request in the network panel and the job progress in the pane.</step>
      </steps>
      <expected>The request is issued to the BFF with the same shape and headers as before the change, and the pane reflects job progress. A malformed init object or a dropped header would indicate an `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` fix applied at the wrong level (declaration versus call site).</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="Word host adapter still extracts the document after type remediation">
      <steps>
        <step>Load the Word add-in in a dev build (`npm run start:word`) and open the pane on a document.</step>
        <step>Run a save and observe that document content is extracted and transmitted.</step>
      </steps>
      <expected>Behaviour is identical to before the task — including the known-broken paths, which this task does not fix. The point of this test is to prove nothing changed, not that anything improved. Any change in extraction behaviour means the adapter seam moved, which is task 010's job, not this one's.</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="ADR-021 dark mode via Office theme toggle">
      <steps>
        <step>With the pane open, switch the Office host theme from light to dark.</step>
        <step>Observe the pane, then reload it and observe again.</step>
        <step>Switch back to light and repeat.</step>
      </steps>
      <expected>The pane follows the Office theme in both directions via the existing `useOfficeTheme` / `useTheme` hooks, with legible text in both themes and no element retaining a light-mode background in dark mode. This task edits no JSX, so any change here would indicate an unintended edit (ADR-021).</expected>
    </test>
  </ui-tests>

  <tools>
    <tool name="npm">`npm install --legacy-peer-deps --no-audit --no-fund` if needed; `npm run typecheck`; `npm run build` (production — there is no build:prod); `npm test`; `npm run start:word` / `npm run start:outlook` for the ui-tests.</tool>
